Fillets: the walk round the vertex at a rounding's end through a face
met at a point (mitcad#236).

PerformIntersectionAtEnd walks from the contact's edge round a face of
the stripe to the vertex and takes, at each vertex passed, the face across
the next edge and the common edge of that face and the one before. Where
more than three faces meet at such a vertex, those two faces share no
edge: a cavity's ceiling and a rib's top under a groove along the wall,
coplanar and meeting at the point where the groove's side crosses the
rib's side; the corner failed ("Failed to find edge."). The fillet's end
passes from the one to the other through the face between them at that
point, the face beyond growing over it.

- The faces round that vertex between the two (away from the stripe's
  face) are put between them as faces at the end, each after the edge it
  shares with the face before it (ChFi3d_FacesBetweenAtVertex), where the
  two faces share no edge at all.
- A face between the first and the last that the fillet crosses at a
  single point gets no curve: the crossing with its first edge is the
  crossing with its last; its last edge is prolonged up to that point as
  edges are where the fillet crosses their faces' intersection beyond
  them, its orientation on the face that of the edge it prolongs.
  There, the vertex the prolongation starts from is the nearest of the
  extrema on the intersection curve (an ellipse's farthest points came
  first), the prolongation the short way on a periodic curve.
- The edge the fillet crosses from the first face, with one end on each
  of the stripe's faces, is cut on the side of the end where the walk
  reached it (the far end lying on the other stripe face as well).
- An OnSame end's last (or first) edge, between the last face at the end
  and the stripe's face that runs on past the vertex, is prolonged up to
  the contact on that face as 0026 prolongs it where one contact lies off
  any edge (the contact here lies on that edge's curve past its end).

